    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-old man presents with progressive ascending motor weakness and areflexia over the past 3 days. A lumbar puncture is performed, and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis reveals:<br>• Protein level: <b>187 mg/dL</b> (normal: 15-45)<br>• White blood cell count: <b>4 cells/uL</b> (normal: 0-5)<br><br>What is the term for this CSF pattern, and what is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Albuminocytologic dissociation, indicating Guillain-Barre syndrome", "Lymphocytic pleocytosis, indicating viral encephalitis", "Polymorphonuclear pleocytosis, indicating bacterial meningitis", "Xanthochromia, indicating subarachnoid hemorrhage", "Oligoclonal bands, indicating Multiple Sclerosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) finding of an elevated protein level in the presence of a normal white blood cell count is known as **albuminocytologic dissociation**. This is the classic hallmark of **Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS)** (acute inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy), which typically presents as progressive, symmetric, ascending muscle weakness and areflexia, often following a gastrointestinal (e.g., Campylobacter jejuni) or respiratory infection. The elevated protein is due to severe inflammation of the myelin sheaths of nerve roots.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-old gentleman with type 1 diabetes is brought to the emergency department unresponsive. Their arterial blood gas (ABG) shows:<br>• pH: <b>7.18</b><br>• pCO2: <b>21 mmHg</b><br>• HCO3: <b>9 mEq/L</b><br><br>Serum sodium is <b>139 mEq/L</b> and chloride is <b>97 mEq/L</b>. What is the acid-base disturbance, and what is the expected respiratory compensation?', '["Mixed high anion gap metabolic acidosis and respiratory acidosis", "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis", "High anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation", "Primary metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation", "Primary respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pH is low (<7.35), indicating an acidemia. The low HCO3 indicates a metabolic acidosis. The anion gap is calculated as Na - (Cl + HCO3), which is elevated (>12), confirming a high anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAGMA), classic for Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA). Using Winter''s formula (expected pCO2 = 1.5 * HCO3 + 8 +/- 2) to evaluate respiratory compensation: the measured pCO2 falls directly within the expected range, indicating appropriate respiratory compensation (hyperventilation/Kussmaul breathing) and no secondary respiratory disturbance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-old lady presents with acute, severe pain, swelling, and erythema in the right first metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint. Arthrocentesis is performed, and synovial fluid examination under polarized light microscopy demonstrates needle-shaped crystals that are strongly negatively birefringent (bright yellow when parallel to the slow vibration axis of the compensator). What is the most likely composition of these crystals, and what is the diagnosis?', '["Monosodium urate crystals, indicating acute gouty arthritis", "Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) crystals, indicating pseudogout", "Calcium oxalate crystals, indicating end-stage renal disease arthritis", "Hydroxyapatite crystals, indicating calcific tendinitis", "Cholesterol crystals, indicating rheumatoid arthritis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Synovial fluid analysis showing **needle-shaped, strongly negatively birefringent** crystals under polarized light is the pathognomonic diagnostic hallmark of **acute gouty arthritis**. These crystals are composed of **monosodium urate**. Gout classically presents as a painful, erythematous monoarthritis affecting the first MTP joint (podagra). In contrast, pseudogout (CPPD) crystals are rhomboid-shaped and weakly positively birefringent (blue when parallel to the compensator).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 58-old lady undergoes a fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy of a 2.1 cm solid thyroid nodule. Histopathologic examination of the aspirate reveals cells with large nuclei, fine chromatin, prominent nucleoli, and characteristic intranuclear inclusions, along with ''Orphan Annie eye'' nuclei and scattered Psammoma bodies. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Thyroid lymphoma", "Medullary thyroid carcinoma", "Papillary thyroid carcinoma", "Follicular thyroid carcinoma", "Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This biopsy description is classic for **papillary thyroid carcinoma**, the most common type of thyroid malignancy. Pathognomonic histopathologic features include **''Orphan Annie eye'' nuclei** (ground-glass appearance of nuclei due to optical clearing), **intranuclear pseudoinclusions**, and **Psammoma bodies** (concentric lamellar calcifications). Papillary thyroid carcinoma has an excellent prognosis and is classically treated with surgical resection followed by radioactive iodine therapy if indicated.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-old gentleman presents with generalized edema, severe hypoalbuminemia, and massive proteinuria (>5 g/24h). A renal biopsy is performed. Under light microscopy, the glomeruli appear entirely normal. Immunofluorescence is negative. Electron microscopy, however, demonstrates diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Minimal change disease", "Diabetic nephropathy", "Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)", "Membranous nephropathy", "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The triad of massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, and generalized edema defines nephrotic syndrome. In a patient with normal light microscopy, negative immunofluorescence, and **diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes on electron microscopy**, the diagnosis is **minimal change disease (MCD)**. MCD is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children but can also occur in adults (sometimes associated with NSAID use or Hodgkin lymphoma). It is highly responsive to corticosteroid therapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-old female is evaluated for progressive dyspnea and dullness to percussion at the left lung base. Diagnostic thoracentesis is performed, and pleural fluid analysis yields the following results:<br>• Pleural fluid protein: <b>3.5 g/dL</b><br>• Serum protein: <b>7.0 g/dL</b><br>• Pleural fluid LDH: <b>430 U/L</b><br>• Serum LDH: <b>217 U/L</b> (normal upper limit: 90 U/L)<br><br>What is the most appropriate classification of this pleural effusion, and what is its most likely underlying etiology?', '["Transudate, secondary to congestive heart failure", "Exudate, secondary to a parapneumonic effusion or malignancy", "Transudate, secondary to liver cirrhosis with hepatic hydrothorax", "Exudate, secondary to nephrotic syndrome", "Chylothorax, secondary to thoracic duct disruption"]'::jsonb, 1, 'According to Light''s criteria, a pleural effusion is classified as an **exudate** if it meets at least one of the following: (1) Pleural fluid protein / Serum protein ratio > 0.5, (2) Pleural fluid LDH / Serum LDH ratio > 0.6, or (3) Pleural fluid LDH > 2/3 the upper limit of normal for serum LDH. Here, the protein ratio is >0.5 and the LDH ratio is >0.6, indicating an exudative effusion. Common causes of exudative effusions include parapneumonic effusions (infection), malignancies, pulmonary embolisms, and tuberculosis. Transudative effusions are caused by systemic factors like congestive heart failure, cirrhosis, or nephrotic syndrome.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-old male is brought to the emergency department due to a 24-hour history of severe headache, high fever, and altered mental status. On physical examination, they have neck stiffness and a positive Brudzinski sign. Lumbar puncture is performed, and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis reveals:<br>• Opening pressure: <b>331 mm H2O</b> (normal: 50-180)<br>• White blood cell count: <b>8904 /uL</b> (normal: 0-5) with 92% polymorphonuclear leukocytes<br>• Protein: <b>226 mg/dL</b> (normal: 15-45)<br>• Glucose: <b>31 mg/dL</b> (serum glucose is 98 mg/dL)<br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute bacterial meningitis", "Multiple sclerosis exacerbation", "Viral aseptic meningitis", "Guillain-Barre syndrome", "Fungal or tuberculous meningitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This CSF profile is highly classic for **acute bacterial meningitis**, characterized by: (1) a markedly elevated opening pressure, (2) severe polymorphonuclear (neutrophilic) pleocytosis, (3) a significantly elevated protein level due to blood-brain barrier disruption, and (4) a markedly reduced glucose level (typically <40 mg/dL or a CSF-to-serum glucose ratio <0.4) because bacteria utilize glucose for energy. Empirical treatment with intravenous cephalosporins (e.g., ceftriaxone), vancomycin, and corticosteroids (dexamethasone) should be initiated immediately.'),
