    (v_topic_id, 'A 41-old lady is evaluated for progressive dyspnea and dullness to percussion at the left lung base. Diagnostic thoracentesis is performed, and pleural fluid analysis yields the following results:<br>• Pleural fluid protein: <b>3.5 g/dL</b><br>• Serum protein: <b>5.6 g/dL</b><br>• Pleural fluid LDH: <b>234 U/L</b><br>• Serum LDH: <b>186 U/L</b> (normal upper limit: 90 U/L)<br><br>What is the most appropriate classification of this pleural effusion, and what is its most likely underlying etiology?', '["Transudate, secondary to congestive heart failure", "Exudate, secondary to a parapneumonic effusion or malignancy", "Chylothorax, secondary to thoracic duct disruption", "Exudate, secondary to nephrotic syndrome", "Transudate, secondary to liver cirrhosis with hepatic hydrothorax"]'::jsonb, 1, 'According to Light''s criteria, a pleural effusion is classified as an **exudate** if it meets at least one of the following: (1) Pleural fluid protein / Serum protein ratio > 0.5, (2) Pleural fluid LDH / Serum LDH ratio > 0.6, or (3) Pleural fluid LDH > 2/3 the upper limit of normal for serum LDH. Here, the protein ratio is >0.5 and the LDH ratio is >0.6, indicating an exudative effusion. Common causes of exudative effusions include parapneumonic effusions (infection), malignancies, pulmonary embolisms, and tuberculosis. Transudative effusions are caused by systemic factors like congestive heart failure, cirrhosis, or nephrotic syndrome.'),
